---
/**
 * Rewrite 2026-08 from owner-supplied project facts. Self-directed status
 * stated up front; research language matched to reality; two claim-titled
 * decision blocks; honest spec-project outcome. Media reduced 7 → 5.
 */
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import ContentBlock from '../../components/ContentBlock.astro';
import ContentMedia from '../../components/ContentMedia.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Idealista — self-directed redesign case study · Jury Alyavdin"
  description="A self-directed Idealista redesign that expresses the brand through structure, not photography — the portfolio piece that started hiring conversations."
  path="/casestudy/idealista.html"
  h1="Making Idealista feel like its brand without slowing the search"
  avatarImage="/casestudy/idealista/logo.jpg"
  orbitTop="Idealista"
  orbitBottom="Real Estate Marketplace"
  orbitTopOffset="12%"
  orbitBottomOffset="62%"
  glow="#d8f848"
  heroImage="/casestudy/idealista/hero.png"
  details={[
    { label: 'Platform', value: 'Web' },
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Product Designer' },
    { label: 'Date', value: '2023' },
  ]}
>
  <ContentBlock title="Overview">
    <p>
      Nobody at Idealista asked for this redesign. I made it in 2023 as a self-directed project
      during a job search: the
      research, the information architecture, and the UI are all my own work. The brief I set
      myself was narrow: make the interface feel like the Idealista brand without making the
      property search any slower. The concept never shipped and was never going to. But it did
      what I built it for — it started hiring conversations.
    </p>
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Problem">
    <p>
      The brand had more character than the product let on. Idealista's identity is distinctive —
      the pixel wordmark, the lime green — but the 2023 interface read as generic listings
      machinery. The homepage was a search box over a stock photo. It greeted a first-time
      visitor and a daily searcher with the same screen.
    </p>
    <p>
      The trap is the obvious fix. A brand-led visual refresh is what a distinctive brand
      invites, and it taxes exactly what a search tool must protect: speed. Expressing the brand
      and keeping the search fast pull in opposite directions, and reconciling them is the whole
      project.
    </p>
    <p>The live site at the time made the case on its own — the brand reduced to a colored bar and a stock hero:</p>
    <ContentMedia src="/casestudy/idealista/current.webp" alt="Idealista's live 2023 homepage: a lime bar and a plain search box over a stock photo" width={2880} height={1400} />
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="A flat search is a race, so the map beat a longer list">
    <p>
      I kept the research honest and small: four to six people I knew who were mid-search, plus
      property viewings I attended in person alongside agents. Informal by design — enough
      contact with the real thing to be corrected by it.
    </p>
    <p>
      It corrected me fast. One searcher put it roughly this way: if you don't call within the
      hour, the flat is gone. At one viewing, the agent walked a whole group of candidates
      through the flat at once, and decisions had to happen on the spot — there was no taking it
      home to compare. And in Spain the listed price is only the opening number: agency fees and
      deposits sit behind it, so comparing listings line by line misleads as much as it informs.
    </p>
    <p>
      A search that works like that needs orientation more than volume. The results page fuses a
      permanent map with a compact listing column — the pairing Airbnb had already proven — so
      every result stays anchored to its place in the city. The alternative I rejected was the
      classic full-width list. It fits more flats per screen, but each one shows a price you
      can't fully trust in a location you can't see.
    </p>
    <p>In the concept, the map never leaves the screen and prices sit where the flats actually are:</p>
    <ContentMedia src="/casestudy/idealista/search-experience.png" alt="Search concept pairing a compact listing column with a permanent map and price pins" width={2880} height={1800} />
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Density expressed the brand better than photography">
    <p>
      My first pass went the other way. I built out deep filtering — more controls, more
      precision — then dropped most of it once I realized people don't compose careful queries.
      They scan and react. The interface had to reward scanning: one row per flat, calm columns,
      the deciding facts where the eye lands first.
    </p>
    <p>
      That call settled the brand question too. Photography is the default way to express a
      brand this visual, and photo-led cards cost exactly what the race can't spare — listings
      per glance. So the brand is expressed through structure instead: Idealista's lime and its
      blocky monospace type, set in dense, quiet layouts. I kept the product's identity and
      restructured the path through it; a full visual overhaul would have read as a new skin on
      the same confusion. Contact actions got the same restraint: they sit on the listing, where
      deciding happens, and stay out of the way while people browse.
    </p>
    <p>The property page moves what a decision needs — price, specs, floor plan, location — into the first screen:</p>
    <ContentMedia src="/casestudy/idealista/property-details.png" alt="Property page concept with price, key specs, floor plan, and mini-map in one view" width={2880} height={1800} />

    <div style={`height: var(--space-block-lg)`} />

    <p>The brand does the work a hero photograph usually does:</p>
    <ContentMedia src="/casestudy/idealista/brand-interface.png" alt="Homepage concept: lime brand block, pixel wordmark, photo mosaic, search field up front" width={2880} height={1936} />

    <div style={`height: var(--space-block-lg)`} />

    <p>
      The homepage also stops treating everyone the same. A first visit gets an explainer of
      what Idealista is; a returning searcher gets saved searches and fresh picks first.
    </p>
    <p>Both variants exist in the concept — one introduces the service, one resumes the search:</p>
    <ContentMedia src="/casestudy/idealista/personalised-journeys.png" alt="Two homepage variants side by side, a first-time explainer and a returning user's dashboard" width={2920} height={1892} />

    <div style={`height: var(--space-block-lg)`} />

    <p>
      Those two homepages are the visible end of two paths I laid out separately. A newcomer has
      to be told what Idealista is before being asked to search; someone mid-hunt has already
      answered that question and wants their last search back.
    </p>
    <p>The first-time path spends its early steps explaining, and only then puts the person into a search:</p>
    <a href="/casestudy/idealista/first-time.png" aria-label="Open the full-size first-time path diagram">
      <ContentMedia src="/casestudy/idealista/first-time.png" alt="Six-step path for a new visitor: landing, discovery, guided tour, search, overview, detail" width={2600} height={1416} />
          <span class="media-note">Open full size</span>
    </a>

    <div style={`height: var(--space-block-lg)`} />

    <p>The returning path skips all of it — saved properties and recent searches are the landing screen:</p>
    <a href="/casestudy/idealista/returning-users.png" aria-label="Open the full-size returning-user path diagram">
      <ContentMedia src="/casestudy/idealista/returning-users.png" alt="Five-step path for a returning searcher, opening on favourites and a resumable search" width={2600} height={1480} />
          <span class="media-note">Open full size</span>
    </a>
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Never shipped, still did its job">
    <p>
      Framed honestly: this is a self-directed concept. Nobody commissioned it and it never
      shipped. What it proves is the argument itself — a brand can be expressed in structure,
      color, and type without taxing a search that behaves like a race. As a portfolio piece it
      did its job and started the hiring conversations it was built for.
    </p>
    <p>
      The trade-off I would name first: the permanent map costs listing density. Every screen
      shows fewer results than a classic list would, and the squeeze is worst on small phones —
      which is where most searching happens. If I came back to this, that tension is where I
      would start.
    </p>
  </ContentBlock>
</CaseStudyLayout>
